    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a proposal for a new high-density residential complex in a mid-sized metro area. Can you give me a summary of the typical arguments people use against high-density zoning? I need to know what we're going to be up against during the public hearing phase.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Common arguments against high-density zoning often revolve around concerns for increased traffic congestion, overburdened local infrastructure like schools and utilities, and the potential loss of neighborhood character or \"right to the sunlight.\" Opponents typically fear that taller buildings will block views and natural light for existing single-family homes, leading to social displacement of long-time residents. Additionally, there is a persistent belief that high density renders the area unappealing for families or local businesses due to perceived noise and safety issues.\nAddressing these concerns proactively with strong data on improved public transit adoption and targeted community green space inclusion can strengthen your proposal.",
      "source": "target",
